 \\]\n   - Substitute the values into the formula:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Reaction Rate} = -\\frac{0.050 \\, \\text{M}}{10 \\, \\text{s}}\n     \\]\n   - Perform the calculation:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Reaction Rate} = -0.005 \\, \\text{M/s}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Final Reaction Rate**: The calculated reaction rate is \\(-0.005 \\, \\text{M/s}\\).\n\n**Misaligned Example**:\nIf the numbers were misaligned, the calculation might look like this:\n\\[\n\\text{Reaction Rate} = -\\frac{0.100 \\, \\text{M} - 0.050 \\, \\text{M}}{10 \\, \\text{s}}\n\\]\n\nWithout proper alignment, the calculation might be:\n\\[\n\\text{Reaction Rate} = -\\frac{0.100 \\, \\text{M} - 0.050 \\, \\text{M}}{10 \\, \\text{s}} = -\\frac{0.050 \\, \\text{M}}{10 \\, \\text{s}} = -0.005 \\, \\text{M/s}\n\\]\n\nHowever, if the numbers were misaligned as:\n\\[\n\\text{Reaction Rate} = -\\frac{0.100 \\, \\text{M} - 0.050 \\, \\text{M}}{10 \\, \\text{s}}\n\\]\n\nThe calculation might be:\n\\[\n\\text{Reaction Rate} = -\\frac{0.100 \\, \\text{M} - 0.050 \\, \\text{M}}{10 \\, \\text{s}} = -\\frac{0.050 \\, \\text{M}}{10 \\, \\text{s}} = -0.005 \\, \\text{M/s}\n\\]\n\nThis misalignment would lead to an incorrect reaction rate of \\(-0.005 \\, \\text{M/s}\\), which is significantly different from the correct value.\n\n**Correct Alignment**:\n\\[\n\\text{Reaction Rate} = -\\frac{0.100 \\, \\text{M} - 0.050 \\, \\text{M}}{10 \\, \\text{s}}\n\\]\n\nWith proper alignment, the calculation is straightforward and accurate, ensuring that the reaction rate is correctly determined.\n\nIn this example, aligning the numbers by their place values is crucial for maintaining the correct order of operations and ensuring the accuracy of the reaction rate calculation. This practice is essential in scientific calculations to avoid errors and ensure reliable results.\n\nThe importance of this practice is evident in scientific calculations, where precision is critical. Proper alignment ensures that all digits are correctly placed, f