h the same number of rows but different numbers of columns:\n\n- Matrix \\(A\\) with dimensions \\(3 \\times 2\\):\n  \\[\n  A = \\begin{pmatrix}\n  1 & 2 \\\\\n  3 & 4 \\\\\n  5 & 6\n  \\end{pmatrix}\n  \\]\n\n- Matrix \\(B\\) with dimensions \\(3 \\times 3\\):\n  \\[\n  B = \\begin{pmatrix}\n  7 & 8 & 9 \\\\\n  10 & 11 & 12 \\\\\n  13 & 14 & 15\n  \\end{pmatrix}\n  \\]\n\nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) do not have the same number of columns, they cannot be added directly. However, if we were to pad \\(A\\) with zeros to match the dimensions of \\(B\\), we would get:\n\n- Padded matrix \\(A'\\) with dimensions \\(3 \\times 3\\):\n  \\[\n  A' = \\begin{pmatrix}\n  1 & 2 & 0 \\\\\n  3 & 4 & 0 \\\\\n  5 & 6 & 0\n  \\end{pmatrix}\n  \\]\n\nNow, we can add \\(A'\\) and \\(B\\):\n\n\\[\nA' + B = \\begin{pmatrix}\n1 & 2 & 0 \\\\\n3 & 4 & 0 \\\\\n5 & 6 & 0\n\\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix}\n7 & 8 & 9 \\\\\n10 & 11 & 12 \\\\\n13 & 14 & 15\n\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}\n1+7 & 2+8 & 0+9 \\\\\n3+10 & 4+11 & 0+12 \\\\\n5+13 & 6+14 & 0+15\n\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}\n8 & 10 & 9 \\\\\n13 & 15 & 12 \\\\\n18 & 20 & 15\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the matrices \\(A'\\) and \\(B\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 8 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 13 & 15 & 12 \\\\ 18 & 20 & 15 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nIn summary, the standard algorithm for addition requires matrices to have the same dimensions. If they do not, you can pad the shorter matrix with zeros to match the dimensions of the longer matrix, or handle the situation differently based on the context.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! When adding matrices with the same number of rows but different numbers of columns, the standard algorithm for addition cannot be directly applied because the matrices do not have the same dimensions. However, if the matrices are square (i.e., the same number of rows and columns), you can add them by adding corresponding elements. Here's the step-by-step process for adding the matrices \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)